at one of the things  that was  really uh written about after that was  the loss of so much of the of an elderly  population that  it felt like they had lost their memory  and their wisdom all at once  and i think you know we're seeing  similar patterns here um hopefully never  on the scale of  that one but um i wonder if you have any  thoughts on  um on losing so much of this memory and  and wisdom  yeah i mean you know in our culture the  wet in the west we  tend not to honor our elders in quite  the same way we do in other cultures  now we're be maybe and until we've lost  what they have to offer us  also people get old without becoming  elders right  um i think one of the things that we  have to look at  you know i i cut my teeth on the aids  epidemic  and you know we now have  millions millions and millions and  millions of people  that have died of hiv and aids and one  of the things that happened in the  beginning was  that there were certain groups of people  that were disposable  gay men ib drug users sex workers  they were not considered valuable  and i think we have to look and see are  we having that idea about our elders now  are we valuing what they have to are we  really taking care of them are we giving  them all the protection that they  deserve  that's a question that's emerging for me  in this pandemic  what happens if we lose institutional  memory what happens if we lose the  wisdom that comes  from generations before us i think  that's not a new normal that i want to  move toward yeah you know it's  especially becoming a question as  people are getting frustrated with  quarantine um and  you know you increasingly hear these  thoughts of like i don't care if i get  it  and um yeah but i care if my  elders get it you know exactly we've got  to think about  our elders and not just elders but  people who have other  underlying conditions this is just  mutual respect actually it's not a  political question  it's a question of heart actually  at least in my mind yeah indeed  uh and that's a good transition to uh  stephen hubbard's question  uh came in um what can we do to  encourage  society's leaders to speak openly and  honestly about death have you had any  luck in this  space depends on the leaders  uh i mean of course i've been speaking  about this  a lot since the beginning of the  pandemic with anybody who'll listen  and i think it's important to recognize  that there are good leaders  there are smart people out there i mean  and there are  people who are lacking in empathy um  you know i i would point to fauci who  did some good work during the hiv  epidemic as well i think he's been  level-headed  and um a calm voice and i'm glad he's in  the room even though  not everybody in the room listens to him  um  i'm not so much for shaming people and  and only reacting with anger i'm  much more a person who likes to  move from being a good example about  what's possible  and i think yes it would be great if our  leaders spoke  positively about this and we can do this  you know we're the ones we've been  waiting  for we can do this leaders are no  leaders  we can make this choice we can take care  of each other this is in our bones  and um and it's now our responsibility  yeah um mary  uh asks um  for some input for uh caregivers it  might be that she's  one of them um but who have  access to you know who may only have  access to  zoom or some other telehealth thing at  this time  have you seen some experience with this  um of  these last experiences really being  mediated by technology  now or yeah i mean it's happening a lot  and thank god we have it you know i mean  isn't it remarkable xander that we have  this they didn't have this in 1918  yeah and you know that we can have  you know these kind of conversations and  still be safe  and keep our loved ones safe that's  really that's important  so yeah sure it's happening all the time  i am speaking to nurses and docs who are  working in skilled nursing facilities  and large medical centers  and yes they can be very rushed and yes  sometimes they can only give the most  cursory  information but i also know nurses that  have stayed out long after their shift  from  holding up an ipad to an elder so that  their family can be with them  i'm happy that we have the technology to  do that and it's even translating into  after death rituals uh funerals and and  memorials that  um people are conducting over zoom  uh it's maybe less than ideal but it's a  way of marking  um this kind of loss yeah  uh ryan phelan um who's  watching online um is asking uh  what what does make a good elder  well that's a whole nother um webinar  we'll have to come back and do that one  but you know she could turn to the guy  next to her you know he'd probably have  something wise to say about this  um you know i think it's somebody who's  learned from their lived experience  who the elders that i know aren't just  those with good theories they've fallen  down  many times you know in zen we have that  expression fall down seven times get up  eight  and i think that's one of the  characteristics that goes when elders an  elder is one who also  knows her heart you know who  understands that that mind can discern  and distinguish clearly  but the heart reveals what's true and  i think wise elders have wise hearts  and so these are just some of the  qualities of course and  we could name a number of others but um  we've all had some contact with the wise  elder somewhere in our life i'm sure  yeah indeed  and brett cox asks  how would you change modern health care  in order to approach palliative care  better what's where are the gaps that  you see there  oh boy um there's quite a few  um well one of the things that  palliative care and hospice care  have in common is that they they  emphasize comfort they emphasize  really not leaving people in intractable  pain  so managing pain and controlling  symptoms are  common to both of those schools of  medicine  hospice care of course is specific to  people who find themselves in the final  six months of life palliative care  doesn't necessarily have that  limitation um  the other thing that happens in both  those systems is everybody works as a  team  and so there's no lone wolfs working no  physician or nurse are working as a lone  wolf  so there's a kind of balance that can be  brought forward an equanimity that can  come forward  from the team from different  perspectives  those are things that i think we could  benefit from in the rest of medicine  [Music]  well let's let's let's just say that if  we did those things that'd be great  yeah be nice to start somewhere yeah um  and um i'm gonna ask just we're gonna  wrap up here pretty soon  um but it's been really great to do this  live um  tim van loan asked i thought it was  a helpful uh closing question  um that these invitations are really  powerful  and helpful um but he could also see  losing them  in in the distractions of life and are  there  is there a way that you suggest to help  keep them at heart  well i think when we let them drop into  our hearts in a way  they become guideposts for us they  become orienting  they become our compass they help us to  orient so that we don't lose track of  them  you know i sit on my meditation cushion  every day  because it stabilizes me it helps me to  regulate but it also  orients me toward my day find some  simple practices  which help you do that whether it's take  one of these precepts or one of these  invitations  or something else that orients you i  have a really simple practice right now  that i'm doing  i go to bed at night and i lie in bed  and sometimes i'm quite scared and  sometimes because of my strokes i have  physical problems and i put my hand on  my heart  and i try to remember the people who are  now in pain  alone at night and i'm very bad at  self-compassion but  compassion emerges with just that  reflection and it spills over onto me  and in the morning i get up in the  morning and i put my hand on my heart  again and i say okay  i choose love today love what would you  have me do today  and again it stabilizes me and guides me  and it's not california these are  central practices in my life that i  think can help orient us toward  living a kinder more compassionate wise  life  yeah keep it simple  thank you and i guess this is a good  moment for me to also mention you know  that  the uh the reason or at least the timing  of the last talk coincided with uh the  release of your amazing book that um  that also covers these topics so people  do want to learn more  i would i would highly recommend it it's  been helpful in my household  um and i i  wrote down a quote during your talk that  um that i feel  is important to leave on and uh this  and i think a very long now quote which  is that the past and the future are just  stories  what is happening now is the only real  experience or true experience i can't  remember exactly how you said it  but uh i think that's a really powerful  and helpful quote  for right now um thank you frank this  has been  really so helpful xander thank you and  thank you too long now for doing this  i mean it's a big gift to the world so  thank you for doing this and all the  other work of course you do all the time  thank you thank you guys um thank you  all for joining and uh  see you at the next event i hope thank  you bye-bye  [Music]

